          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-garmin">Garmin</span></td><td>{s['brand_counts']['Garmin']:,}</td><td>{pct(s['brand_counts']['Garmin']/s['total_listings']*100)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_median'],2)}</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-samsung">Samsung</span></td><td>{s['brand_counts']['Samsung']:,}</td><td>{pct(s['brand_counts']['Samsung']/s['total_listings']*100)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_median'],2)}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">Apple holds two-thirds of listings, but Garmin commands the highest median price among the three. Samsung shows the widest price variance, driven by a small number of ultra-high-priced outliers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Condition & Shipping Mix</h3>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Listings</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>New</td><td>{s['condition_counts']['New']:,}</td><td>{pct(s['condition_counts']['New']/s['total_listings']*100)}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Used</td><td>{s['condition_counts']['Used']:,}</td><td>{pct(s['condition_counts']['Used']/s['total_listings']*100)}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Other / Refurbished</td><td>{s['condition_counts']['Other']:,}</td><td>{pct(s['condition_counts']['Other']/s['total_listings']*100)}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p><strong>Shipping reach varies sharply by brand.</strong> Only {pct(apple_ship)} of Apple sellers offer worldwide shipping, versus {pct(garmin_ship)} for Garmin and {pct(samsung_ship)} for Samsung. This has direct implications for cross-border arbitrage: Garmin and Samsung sellers are already structurally positioned for international resale.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: BRAND PRICING -->
<section id="brand-pricing">
  <h2>2. Brand-Level Pricing & Value Retention</h2>
  <p>Resale value retention differs materially across brands. Garmin maintains the tightest price distribution with the highest median, while Samsung exhibits extreme right-tail skew — its mean is <strong>37% above its median</strong>, signaling outlier-driven volatility.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['brand_pricing']}" alt="Brand Pricing Distribution"></div>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Brand</th><th>Median</th><th>Mean</th><th>Q1</th><th>Q3</th><th>IQR</th><th>Min</th><th>Max</th><th>Std Dev</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-apple">Apple</span></td><td>${fmt(s['apple_median'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_mean'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_q1'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_q3'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_iqr'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_min'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_max'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_std'],2)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-garmin">Garmin</span></td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_median'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_mean'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_q1'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_q3'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_iqr'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_min'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_max'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_std'],2)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-samsung">Samsung</span></td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_median'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_mean'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_q1'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_q3'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_iqr'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_min'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_max'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_std'],2)}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Garmin's IQR of ${fmt(s['garmin_iqr'],2)} is the widest in absolute terms, but its coefficient of variation (std/mean) is the lowest at {fmt(s['garmin_std']/s['garmin_mean']*100,1)}%, indicating predictable, premium pricing. Samsung's CV is {fmt(s['samsung_std']/s['samsung_mean']*100,1)}% — nearly 3× higher — making inventory valuation riskier.
  </div>

  <h3>New vs. Used Price Premium</h3>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['condition_premium']}" alt="Condition Premium"></div>
    <div>
      <p>Apple is the only brand where "New" commands a consistent and meaningful premium over "Used" listings across the full dataset.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Brand</th><th>New Median</th><th>Used Median</th><th>Premium</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-apple">Apple</span></td><td>${fmt(s['apple_new_median'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['apple_used_median'],2)}</td><td style="color:var(--green);font-weight:600">+{pct(s['apple_new_premium'])}</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-garmin">Garmin</span></td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_new_median'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['garmin_used_median'],2)}</td><td style="color:var(--red);font-weight:600">{pct(s['garmin_new_premium'])}</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-samsung">Samsung</span></td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_new_median'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['samsung_used_median'],2)}</td><td style="color:var(--green);font-weight:600">+{pct(s['samsung_new_premium'])}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">Garmin's negative premium is driven by model mix: high-end used fenix and tactix units retain value exceptionally well, compressing the new-used gap. Samsung's near-zero premium reflects weak new-condition pricing power in the resale channel.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: GEOGRAPHIC ARBITRAGE -->
<section id="geographic-arbitrage">
  <h2>3. Geographic Pricing Arbitrage</h2>
  <p>Country-level median prices diverge by up to <strong>${fmt(s['apple_spread'],2)} for Apple</strong>, <strong>${fmt(s['garmin_spread'],2)} for Garmin</strong>, and <strong>${fmt(s['samsung_spread'],2)} for Samsung</strong>. These spreads represent direct margin opportunities for operators with cross-border logistics capability.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['geographic_arbitrage']}" alt="Geographic Arbitrage"></div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3><span class="badge badge-apple">Apple</span> Arbitrage Map</h3>
      {geo_table('Apple')}
      <div class="insight" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Opportunity:</strong> Apple units in the UK trade at a {pct(abs(uk_apple_pct),1)} discount to global median. Sourcing from the UK and reselling into Australia (premium market) yields a theoretical spread of ${fmt(s['apple_spread'],2)} per unit before logistics.</div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3><span class="badge badge-garmin">Garmin</span> Arbitrage Map</h3>
      {geo_table('Garmin')}
      <div class="insight" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Opportunity:</strong> Garmin shows the strongest UK premium at +{pct(uk_garmin_pct,1)}. Japan is the clear low-cost source ({pct(japan_garmin_pct,1)}), making it the optimal sourcing geography for Garmin arbitrage.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:24px">
    <h3><span class="badge badge-samsung">Samsung</span> Arbitrage Map</h3>
    {geo_table('Samsung')}
    <div class="insight" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Opportunity:</strong> Samsung's UK median (${fmt(s['samsung_spread_max_val'],2)}) is {pct(uk_samsung_pct,1)} above global median, while Japan sits at {pct(jp_samsung_pct,1)} below. The Japan→UK route offers the largest per-unit spread among all brand-country pairs in this dataset.</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Arbitrage KPIs</h3>
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi"><div class="kpi-value">${fmt(s['apple_spread'],2)}</div><div class="kpi-label">Apple Geographic Spread</div></div>
