    <div class="kpi"><div class="kpi-value">{s['countries']}</div><div class="kpi-label">Markets Analyzed</div></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: CASE SIZE -->
<section id="case-size">
  <h2>4. Case Size & Price Premium Analysis</h2>
  <p>Case size data is available for {s['case_size_n']:,} listings ({pct(s['case_size_n']/s['total_listings']*100)} of the dataset). The overall correlation between case size and price is weak (<strong>r = {fmt(s['case_size_corr'],3)}</strong>), but brand-specific patterns reveal meaningful segmentation effects.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['case_size']}" alt="Case Size vs Price"></div>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Brand</th><th>Correlation (r)</th><th>Listings with Size</th><th>Mean Case Size</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-apple">Apple</span></td><td>{fmt(s['apple_case_corr'],3)}</td><td>{s['apple_case_n']:,}</td><td>46.2 mm</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-garmin">Garmin</span></td><td>{fmt(s['garmin_case_corr'],3)}</td><td>{s['garmin_case_n']:,}</td><td>45.5 mm</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-samsung">Samsung</span></td><td>{fmt(s['samsung_case_corr'],3)}</td><td>{s['samsung_case_n']:,}</td><td>45.0 mm</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Garmin shows the strongest case-size correlation (r = {fmt(s['garmin_case_corr'],3)}), driven by its product architecture: larger fenix and tactix models are systematically priced higher than smaller Forerunner variants. For Apple and Samsung, case size is not a reliable price predictor — model generation and condition matter far more than physical dimensions.
  </div>

  <h3>Price by Size Bucket</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Size Bucket</th><th>Overall Median</th><th>Apple Median</th><th>Garmin Median</th><th>Samsung Median</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>≤40 mm</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket']['≤40mm'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Apple','≤40mm'),0),2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Garmin','≤40mm'),0),2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Samsung','≤40mm'),0),2)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>41–44 mm</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket']['41-44mm'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Apple','41-44mm'),0),2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Garmin','41-44mm'),0),2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Samsung','41-44mm'),0),2)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>45–48 mm</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket']['45-48mm'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Apple','45-48mm'),0),2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Garmin','45-48mm'),0),2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Samsung','45-48mm'),0),2)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&gt;48 mm</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket']['>48mm'],2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Apple','>48mm'),0),2)}</td><td>${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Garmin','>48mm'),0),2)}</td><td>N/A</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">Samsung has no listings >48 mm in the dataset. Garmin's >48 mm bucket (primarily 50–52 mm fenix/tactix units) commands the highest median of any size-brand combination at ${fmt(s['size_bucket_brand'].get(('Garmin','>48mm'),0),2)}.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: POWER SELLERS -->
<section id="power-sellers">
  <h2>5. Power Seller Behavior & Pricing Patterns</h2>
  <p>The top 5% of sellers by volume ({s['power_seller_count']} sellers) control <strong>{pct(s['power_listings_pct'])} of all listings</strong> — a highly concentrated market. These power sellers operate with structurally different pricing, brand mix, and logistics strategies than casual single-listing sellers.</p>

  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['power_seller']}" alt="Power Seller Analysis"></div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Market Concentration</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Power sellers (top 5%)</td><td>{s['power_seller_count']}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Listings controlled</td><td>{s['power_listings_count']:,} ({pct(s['power_listings_pct'])})</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Avg listings per power seller</td><td>{fmt(s['power_avg_listings'],1)}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Largest seller volume</td><td>{s['top_seller_max']} listings</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Pricing: Power vs. Casual</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Brand</th><th>Power Seller Median</th><th>Casual Seller Median</th><th>Gap</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-apple">Apple</span></td><td>${fmt(power_apple_med,2)}</td><td>${fmt(casual_apple_med,2)}</td><td style="color:var(--green);font-weight:600">+{gap_pct(power_apple_med, casual_apple_med)}</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-garmin">Garmin</span></td><td>${fmt(power_garmin_med,2)}</td><td>${fmt(casual_garmin_med,2)}</td><td style="color:var(--green);font-weight:600">+{gap_pct(power_garmin_med, casual_garmin_med)}</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="badge badge-samsung">Samsung</span></td><td>${fmt(power_samsung_med,2)}</td><td>${fmt(casual_samsung_med,2)}</td><td style="color:var(--green);font-weight:600">+{gap_pct(power_samsung_med, casual_samsung_med)}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Strategic Profile of Power Sellers</h3>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <p><strong>Brand Mix:</strong> Power sellers are dramatically underweight Apple ({pct(s['power_brand_mix']['Apple']*100)} vs. {pct(s['casual_brand_mix']['Apple']*100)} for casual sellers) and overweight Garmin ({pct(s['power_brand_mix']['Garmin']*100)} vs. {pct(s['casual_brand_mix']['Garmin']*100)}). This suggests Garmin's supply chain is more institutionalized — likely due to its presence in outdoor/sports retail channels that liquidate inventory at scale.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p><strong>Condition & Logistics:</strong> Power sellers list <strong>{pct(s['power_condition_mix']['New']*100)} new-condition items</strong> versus only {pct(s['casual_condition_mix']['New']*100)} for casual sellers. They also offer worldwide shipping at {pct(s['power_shipping'])} — more than 5× the casual seller rate ({pct(s['casual_shipping'])}). Power sellers are structurally optimized for cross-border volume.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Power sellers price <strong>{pct(s['power_premium'])} higher</strong> on average than casual sellers, but with far lower variance (especially for Apple and Samsung). They are not undercutting the market — they are extracting a reliability premium. Buyers appear willing to pay more for sellers with scale, consistent stock, and global shipping.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<section id="recommendations">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <h4>1. Prioritize Garmin in Cross-Border Arbitrage</h4>
    <p>Garmin shows the clearest low-cost sourcing geography (Japan at {pct(japan_garmin_pct,1)} below global median) and the highest destination premium (UK at +{pct(uk_garmin_pct,1)}). The Japan→UK route offers a ${fmt(s['garmin_spread'],2)} median spread with lower price volatility than Samsung. Target 45–48 mm and >48 mm fenix/tactix units, which command the highest size premiums.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <h4>2. Exploit UK Apple Discount for Premium Markets</h4>
    <p>Apple listings in the UK trade at a {pct(abs(uk_apple_pct),1)} discount to global median — the deepest discount of any major brand-country pair. With Apple representing 66% of total listings, even a narrow margin on high-volume UK sourcing scales meaningfully. Resell into Australia (+{pct(au_apple_pct,1)}) or South Korea (+{pct(kr_apple_pct,1)}) for maximum spread capture.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <h4>3. Avoid Samsung as a Primary Inventory Focus</h4>
    <p>Samsung's mean-to-median ratio of 1.37 and extreme standard deviation (${fmt(s['samsung_std'],2)}) indicate unpredictable pricing. The brand also shows near-zero new-condition premium (+{pct(s['samsung_new_premium'])}), suggesting weak pricing power in resale. If Samsung is included in inventory, limit exposure to proven SKUs from the UK (premium market) and avoid speculative bulk purchases.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <h4>4. Partner with Power Sellers, Do Not Undercut Them</h4>
    <p>Power sellers control {pct(s['power_listings_pct'])} of listings and price {pct(s['power_premium'])} above casual sellers — yet they still dominate volume. This indicates buyers value reliability over marginal price savings. Rather than competing on price, secondary-market operators should partner with power sellers as consignment or drop-ship partners, leveraging their global shipping infrastructure ({pct(s['power_shipping'])} coverage) and new-condition supply ({pct(s['power_condition_mix']['New']*100)} of their listings).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <h4>5. Condition Strategy Should Be Brand-Specific</h4>
    <p>Apple is the only brand where new-condition inventory justifies a sourcing premium (+{pct(s['apple_new_premium'])} median). For Garmin, used units retain value so well that the new-used gap is negative — focus on high-quality used inventory from Japan and the US. Samsung shows no meaningful condition premium; prioritize cost-per-unit over condition grade.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <h4>6. Case Size Is a Garmin-Specific Sizing Lever</h4>
    <p>With an r = {fmt(s['garmin_case_corr'],3)} correlation, Garmin is the only brand where larger case sizes reliably predict higher prices. For Apple and Samsung, case size adds negligible pricing signal. When sourcing Garmin, actively filter for 47+ mm units; for Apple and Samsung, ignore case size and optimize for model generation and condition instead.</p>
  </div>
</section>

